Do NOT blindly rename existing enum values.

If the existing architecture uses different names, preserve compatibility
and document the canonical mapping.

==================================================
4. EVENT VALIDATION
==================================================

Implement typed event validation.

Validate:

- event type
- eventId
- timestamp
- required fields
- IDs
- lifecycle states
- versions
- actor context where required

Reject malformed events.

Use existing Zod conventions.

Do not silently accept malformed audit events.

==================================================
5. EVENT SANITIZATION
==================================================

This is a SECURITY-CRITICAL requirement.

Event payloads must never leak:

- apiKey
- API keys
- password
- token
- secret
- credential
- pwd
- passphrase
- authorization headers
- access tokens
- refresh tokens

Sanitization must be:

- case-insensitive
- nested
- recursive
- non-mutating
- deterministic

Use or extend the existing canonical sanitizer instead of creating
another incompatible sanitizer.

Ensure secrets cannot leak through:

- event metadata
- error details
- actor metadata
- operation payload
- previous values
- new values

==================================================
6. APPEND-ONLY EVENT LOG
==================================================

Create a typed EventLog contract.

Example conceptual API:

append(event)
appendBatch(events)
getById(eventId)
query(query)
count(query)
latest(...)
health(...)
capabilities(...)

Adapt this to the existing architecture.

The EventLog must be append-only.

Normal consumers must NOT be able to mutate historical events.

No update(event).
No delete(event).

If future retention/deletion is required, model it as a policy rather
than allowing arbitrary mutation of the audit trail.

==================================================
7. EVENT IMMUTABILITY
==================================================

EventLog must protect stored events from mutation.

Test:

- top-level mutation
- nested payload mutation
- metadata mutation
- arrays
- objects
- actor context
- returned query results

Reading an event must never expose the internal mutable object.

==================================================
8. DETERMINISTIC EVENT IDs
==================================================

Event IDs must be unique.

Prefer injected ID generation where appropriate so tests can be
deterministic.

Do NOT use Math.random() inside deterministic test paths.

Where event IDs are derived from content, use a safe deterministic
hash strategy.

Do not sacrifice uniqueness merely for determinism.

==================================================
9. EVENT ORDERING
==================================================

Provide deterministic event ordering.

Support ordering using:

- timestamp
- sequence/order
- eventId as stable tiebreaker

Do not rely solely on timestamps because multiple events may share
the same timestamp.

The EventLog must return the same ordering for the same stored events.

==================================================
10. CORRELATION
==================================================

Support correlation fields so one operation can be traced across events.

Where available:

- requestId
- executionId
- correlationId
- actorId
- memoryId

Do not invent execution IDs where the caller already provides them.

==================================================
11. EVENT QUERY
==================================================

Implement typed deterministic query support.

Useful filters:

- eventType
- memoryId
- actorId
- organizationId
- workspaceId
- projectId
- requestId
- correlationId
- severity
- category
- timestamp range
- version
- lifecycle state

Do not implement arbitrary untyped filter objects.

==================================================
12. PAGINATION
==================================================

Reuse the Sprint 6 pagination philosophy.

Prefer deterministic cursor pagination.

Requirements:

- validated limit
- configured maximum
- stable ordering
- fail-closed invalid cursor
- no duplicate events between pages
- no missing events between pages

Do not duplicate Sprint 6 pagination utilities unnecessarily.

If an existing generic pagination abstraction can be reused,
reuse it.

==================================================
13. EVENT RETENTION
==================================================

Do NOT implement automatic deletion of audit events in this sprint.

Instead create a typed retention policy abstraction if the existing
architecture needs one.

The policy must clearly distinguish:

- active audit records
- expired records
- archival
- legal/security retention

Do not silently delete audit records.

Document unresolved retention decisions.

==================================================
14. REPLAY / RECONSTRUCTION
==================================================

Create a safe event replay abstraction only if it fits the existing
architecture.

Replay must NOT mutate production memory automatically.

Provide a deterministic way to inspect/reconstruct state from events.

If full event-sourced reconstruction would require an architecture
redesign, defer it and document the decision.

Do not turn AG-002 into a full event-sourcing architecture.

==================================================
15. SERVICE INTEGRATION
==================================================

Integrate event logging with existing AG-002 operations where
appropriate.

Review:

- MemoryService
- LifecycleService
- AuthorizationService
- RetrievalService
- ConsolidationService

At minimum, events should correctly represent important state-changing
and security-sensitive operations.

Do not log every internal implementation detail.

Avoid duplicate events.

A denied authorization must not accidentally emit a successful
operation event.

==================================================
16. SECURITY EVENT RULES
==================================================

Ensure:

ALLOW:

authorization allowed
→ AccessAllowed where appropriate
→ operation event only if operation actually succeeds

DENY:

authorization denied
→ AccessDenied / specific denial event
→ no fake successful memory operation event

FAILED:

operation fails
→ failure event only where appropriate
→ error data sanitized

Never emit secrets.

